hey guys Greg knuckles here with string  theory calm back with this installment  of our semi daily Q&amp;A series today's  question is what are those sleep  requirements for a lifter for an athlete  is the typical recommendation of about  seven to eight hours per night enough  you know what are the drawbacks of  sleeping less than that and are there  benefits to sleeping more than that or  is that going to cause some drawbacks  that's an idea a lot of people have so  um first off just just to say this on  the outset this is mainly applicable  well this is primarily applicable to  people who have normal genetics for  sleep there are actually a few genes  that regulate how much you need to sleep  per night and the vast vast majority of  people have the typical allele for those  where a normal night of sleep is about  seven to eight and a half hours per  night there are some people a very small  minority of people like five percent of  the population or fewer that actually  have a gene that lets them for all  intents and purposes sleep faster so  they get as much out of a four to five  hour night of sleep as the typical  person would get out of an eight hour  night of sleep I'm very jealous of those  people and that very well may include  a lot of disproportionately successful  people who report sleeping hardly any at  all at night so those people are out  there but I'm assuming most of the  people I'm talking to you don't fall  into that category and you can't train  yourself to fall in that category  sleeping less and less you don't get  good at getting by on less sleep you  just feel really tired all the time  oh and one other thing to mention before  we get into this if you  are under sleeping you're probably not  the best judge of whether you're under  sleeping or not there was a study done  looking at mental performance with sleep  deprivation had people if I'm  remembering correctly sleeping six hours  per night and then while one group  sleeping six hours per night one group  sleeping I think eight and a half hours  per night and then one group was playing  consecutive all-nighters and the six  hour group they when basically they ran  them through mental tasks and look to  see what their performance actually was  and then ask them how much do you think  your performance has declined and they  they said correctly that they felt their  performance had declined after 24 hours  then by 48 hours if I'm remembering  correctly they said yeah I think I I'm  doing just as well as I did yesterday  you know haven't gotten any worse but  not as good as a full night of sleep and  then same thing after three days of poor  sleep they thought that their  performance was below baseline but  hadn't really declined any more win in  reality their performance had just  declined linearly all three days so they  didn't realize how sleep-deprived they  were they felt that they a kind of  plateaued after one night of bad sleep  when actually after three days of  sleeping six hours per night their  mental performance was about the same as  it would have been if they pulled an  all-nighter so whoo yeah that's that's  just a random aside before we actually  get into the video  okay so first core the the main question  you know are there benefits to sleeping  more and what are the drawbacks to  sleeping less I'll address this the  second part of that question first  because that's what most of the research  thus far is kind of looking at so  there's a lot of research comparing  sleeping a normal amount seven eight and  a half hours or so to sleep being  substantially less than that so they  typically use five and a half to six  hours in those studies as the short  sleeping group and I'll just sum all of  that up very quickly a bunch of bad [ __ ]  happens and no good [ __ ] happens  physical performance goes down mental  performance goes down just become  crankier that's that's not a good thing  if you're a fast sleeper and you can get  by on that that's great for most of us  sleeping at least seven and a half eight  eight and a half hours per night is  going to be dramatically better than  sleeping less than that both in terms of  just how you feel  and then also probably how you'll  respond to training and diet as well  there haven't been that many long-term  studies looking at this partially  because it's not incredibly ethical to  make people sleep less for prolonged  periods of time I am hard to run RCTs on  that because like ethic sports know that  that's a very unhealthy thing and so  it's hard to actually perform research  on that but some things we do know is  there have been relatively short-term  studies but long enough to get a good  idea of what's going on looking at  short-sleeved versus normal amounts of  sleep on body composition in a calorie  deficit and they find that when you're  sleeping less you lose the same amount  of actual weight as when you're sleeping  more when diet is controlled but more of  that weight lost is lean mass and less  of it is fat mass so that's no good  and then in terms of population wide  studies people who sleep less tend to  have more body fat higher BMIs  whereas health outcomes so that would  probably primarily apply to a calorie  surplus or at maintenance so yeah short  answer there if you're sleeping less  than at least seven and a half hours per  night you're probably going to benefit a  lot like a very meaningful amount by you  know trying to get you know trying to  get a bed at least eight hours per night  or so the more interesting question is  are there additional benefits on top of  that to sleeping more than eight hours  per night and this it doesn't have quite  as much research to this point and for a  long time people recommend it against  this even for healthy athletes because  there is some population at the  epidemiological research showing that  people who sleep longer than about eight  eight and a half hours per night tend to  have worse health outcomes than people  who do sleep kind of a more normal  amount but that very well could be and  probably is a case of mistaken causality  so it's not necessarily that sleeping  more is bad for you  what's probably going on there is people  who are already unhealthy are fatigued  and need to sleep more or don't have  jobs and are less busy and can sleep  more and that's also associated  generally with Werth's life outcomes in  general so that's that's a concern a lot  of people have I think that that is  probably just a case of mistaken  causality they're just assuming  correlation is causation when it's  actually not now in terms of actual  controlled research done on sleep  extension there's not too much at this  point most of it is coming out of Sherry  Maas lab at Stanford University and I  will just admit on the front end it's  they haven't looked at anything having  to do with strength hypertrophy power  lifting bodybuilding anything like that  to this point it's been looking  primarily at you know other athletes of  you know the types of sports that more  people in the world care about just  being Frank about it  so they've looked at tennis swimming  basketball and I think football but I  know tennis swimming and basketball and  so they've looked at some stuff that  would primarily be associated more focus  and mental performance so you know  free-throw shooting accuracy three-point  shooting accuracy  serve accuracy and return accuracy in  tennis but they have also looked at just  more pure performance based things so  for the basketball players sprint times  for the swimmers  sprint times over fairly short distances  and they found man I wish I would have  got the exact numbers but pretty  dramatic improvements so we're talking I  like 5 to 8% decreases in sprint times  on average which if you've ever looked  if you've ever worked with athletes  where sprint times are important that's  a huge drop like that's the type of drop  that you'd be looking at for maybe like  a six month to a year long training  cycle and we're talking those are the  benefits associated with about two weeks  of sleep extension so having them go  from sleeping about eight hours per  night to nine to ten hours per night so  like you can't draw a straight line  between sprint times and your lifts in  the gym or hypertrophy I'm not claiming  that for a moment but I am saying that  you know sprint times that's primarily  power output that is closely associated  with force output you're just generally  less mentally fatigued so you will  probably be able to train harder recover  faster  so in general I definitely think to this  point the research is leaning towards if  you can sleep 9 to 10 hours per night if  you have time for that in your schedule  you will probably get additional maybe  substantial benefits from sleeping an  extra hour to per night even if you're  already sleeping a normal amount seven  and a half eight hours per night  typically so that is the video if you're  sleeping five six hours per night now  and you feel like garbage you're  probably not one of those lucky fastest  Leber's definitely sleep more if you can  make time in your schedule get in bed an  extra hour to pronate that is going to  pay big dividends for you if you're  already sleeping seven and a half eight  hours per night and you're training hard  you will probably also get some benefits  from sleeping an extra hour so if you  can fit that into your schedule but it's  probably not going to be a night and day  difference so you know don't freak  yourself out if if that's just not  possible for you but if it is it is  definitely worth a shot over two or  three weeks just see if it makes a  difference for you  just for myself it's something that I I  believe strongly in  from my experiences with it I do  typically sleep about seven to eight  hours per night but the two best  training blocks I've had since just my  initial noob gains in a decade ago when  I started training or whatever since  that point the two best training cycles  I've had once I've already been in a  well trained state were two periods of  my life when I did have just fewer  responsibilities and I could get in bed  an average of about eight and a half to  ten hours per night depending on on the  night of the week and that that did make  at least for me anecdotally a big  difference in terms of my results how  quickly I gained strength and how well I  recovered from training so if that's  something that you do have room in your  schedule to to give a try I think it's I  think it's definitely worth a crack if  you can this was a little bit more of a  family video than normal sleep is  something that I tend to get pretty  passionate about one of the few topics  that I do get really preachy about  because I do think it is very very  important and a lot of times its  importance is understated and it's not  talked about as much because you know  sleeps not as sexy is something like a  cool training program new diet ideas  supplement whatever so I feel like it  doesn't get as much love as it should  but it is a very very important topic  and you know you should try to sleep  probably about as much as you can as  much as your schedule allows so that is  the video if you liked it too please  like and subscribe and if you have a  question you want answered ask it in the  comments below and if it is my favorite  I will pick it and answer it in that  next video alright guys thanks for  watching and until next time hope your  training is great hope you have a great  weekend and I will see you in that next  video
 

